# Remediation — DNS Incident Analysis

## Immediate Resolution Steps

### Step 1 — Check and Restart the DNS Service
SSH into the DNS server and verify whether the service is running:

bash
# Check service status (Linux / systemd)
sudo systemctl status named
sudo systemctl status bind9

# If stopped, restart it
sudo systemctl restart named
# or
sudo systemctl restart bind9

# Verify it is now listening on port 53
sudo ss -ulnp | grep :53

This is the first and most likely fix. A stopped or crashed DNS service is the most common explanation for a consistent port unreachable ICMP error.

---

### Step 2 — Review Firewall Rules
Check whether a recent firewall change is blocking UDP port 53:

bash
# List current iptables rules
sudo iptables -L -n -v

# Check for rules blocking port 53
sudo iptables -L -n | grep 53

# If a blocking rule was added in error, remove it
sudo iptables -D INPUT -p udp --dport 53 -j DROP
```

If using ufw:
bash
sudo ufw status
sudo ufw allow 53/udp

---

### Step 3 — Check Server Resource Usage
Confirm the server did not crash due to resource exhaustion:

bash
# CPU and memory
top
free -h

# Disk usage — a full disk can cause service crashes
df -h

# Check system logs for crash indicators
sudo journalctl -xe | grep named
sudo tail -100 /var/log/syslog

---

## Longer-Term Prevention

### Step 4 — Deploy a Secondary (Backup) DNS Server
A single DNS server is a critical single point of failure. If it goes down, the entire domain becomes unreachable.

Recommendation:
- Configure a secondary DNS server on separate infrastructure
- Use DNS failover so that if the primary is unreachable, queries are routed to the backup
- Cloud DNS services (e.g., AWS Route 53, Cloudflare DNS) provide built-in redundancy

---
 Step 5 — Implement DNS Monitoring & Alerting
The incident was discovered when users started complaining — not when the service first failed. Proactive monitoring would catch this faster.

Recommended checks:
- Set up a synthetic monitor that queries 203.0.113.2:53 every 60 seconds
- Alert on-call if port 53 returns ICMP unreachable or no response within timeout
- Tools: Nagios, Zabbix, Datadog, UptimeRobot (for external monitoring)

